            "type": "reasoning",
            "content": [
              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Grids**\n\nI'm examining these grids with sparse, colored pixels, focusing on how specific pixel locations relate to vertical lines. For train 1, I see pixels at (4,7), (8,5), and (10,1) with associated colors. My output should detail the vertical lines for each pixel's column. But I'm pondering their colors.\n\n\n**Revising Line Intersections**\n\nI've been re-evaluating the behavior of the line intersections. Now I think I've cracked it: horizontal lines use the pixel color and vertical lines use the pixel color. When a horizontal line of a pixel intersects a different pixel's vertical line, the intersection's color is 2 (red). If it intersects its own vertical line, it keeps the original pixel's color. This now seems more solid!\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
            "status": "completed",
            "format": "google-gemini-v1"
          },
          {
            "id": "msg_tmp_98ur3yx32se",
            "content": [
              {
                "annotations": [],
                "text": "An intuitive and clear transformation rule is identified across all examples:\n1. Each non-zero pixel $(r_i, c_i)$ with color $C_i$ projects a full vertical line of color $C_i$ along column $c_i$, and a full horizontal line of color $C_i$ along row $r_i$.\n2. Wherever the horizontal line of one pixel intersects the vertical line of a *different* pixel (i.e. at $(r_i, c_j)$ with $i \\neq j$), the color is set to `2` (red).\n3. The original pixel position $(r_i, c_i)$ retains its own color $C_i$.\n\nPlease provide the test input grid.",
